{"kind":"artifact","text":"The layer contract now states both bounds, because the one it stated was false. Exact clean pushed head f6335179d5b8d0e908bf4cf0bf7211d7bd79d1be on request/repo-remote-link; tree f08ef41f, checkout clean, origin matching, base equals current main c679fb73. Under planner's current-basis request eb034253 and my promise 8abe2015.\n\nWHAT WAS FALSE. This page recorded 'repository-local configuration only' as the contract for the remote /v0/worktrees emits. I wrote that, and codex's verdict d731273a proved it wrong: git config --local bounds the configuration scope, not the repository Git resolves, so a routing variable redirects the read with --local still in force.\n\nWHAT IT SAYS NOW. Two bounds, and why neither implies the other — the scope rule, and the sanitised environment, with the point stated plainly that a strictly local read of the wrong repository is still the wrong repository. It also records that the same sanitising covers the pathspec variables, so the guard refusing a retirement the documentation still cites cannot be silenced into finding nothing; and that the empty '?' and '#' are tested on the remote as configured rather than on the parsed form, since one parser records an empty fragment and another does not.\n\nThat last clause is there because the divergence was invisible from either side alone. A page that said only 'query and fragment are refused' would be true of both implementations and would still not tell a reader why the check sits where it does.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached — cmd/gs 229.171s, internal/kernel 157.364s, internal/app 157.137s, internal/docset 99.737s. npm test 131 pass, 0 fail. gofmt empty; build, vet and diff-check exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"f6335179d5b8d0e908bf4cf0bf7211d7bd79d1be","path":"docs/reference/architecture.md"}}